---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getSites, scoreClass, countryName, categoryLabel } from '../../lib/data';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const sites = getSites();
---
<Layout title="All sites">
  <h1>All sites</h1>
  <p class="lede">Every site in this run, ranked by mean IPTC score. Sites whose <code>robots.txt</code> disallows our crawler are shown but not scored.</p>

  <table class="sortable">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Site</th><th>Country</th><th>Category</th><th>Status</th><th class="num">Images</th><th class="num">% IPTC</th><th class="num">Score</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {sites.map(s => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`${base}/sites/${s.site_id}/`}>{s.site_name}</a></td>
          <td><a href={`${base}/countries/${s.country.toLowerCase()}/`}>{countryName(s.country)}</a></td>
          <td><span class={`pill ${s.category}`}>{categoryLabel(s.category)}</span></td>
          <td>{s.status === 'ok' ? <span>ok</span> : <em>{s.status}</em>}</td>
          <td class="num">{s.images_analysed}</td>
          <td class="num">{s.pct_with_iptc}%</td>
          <td class="num">
            <span class={`score-bar ${scoreClass(s.mean_iptc_score)}`}><span style={`width:${s.mean_iptc_score}%`}></span></span>
            {s.mean_iptc_score.toFixed(1)}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What each status means</h2>
  <dl class="status-glossary">
    <dt><code>ok</code></dt>
    <dd>The crawler made it all the way through: <code>robots.txt</code> allowed our user-agent,
      discovery found articles, and we fetched images. The score and percentage columns reflect
      the sampled images.</dd>

    <dt><code>robots_disallow</code></dt>
    <dd>The site's <code>robots.txt</code> explicitly disallows the <code>Metawatch/2.0</code>
      user-agent (or all user-agents) at the site root. We respect this and skip the rest of the
      crawl, but we still read <code>robots.txt</code> and probe the site-wide AI-preference
      files, so the publisher still appears in the <a href={`${base}/ai-policy/`}>AI prefs</a>
      counts.</dd>

    <dt><code>blocked_by_waf</code></dt>
    <dd>A sitemap or feed request was met by a web-application firewall / bot-management
      challenge that we could positively fingerprint — a vendor signature in the response
      headers (Cloudflare, Akamai, DataDome, Imperva, Sucuri) or a challenge-page body such as
      Cloudflare's <em>“Just a moment…”</em>. The feed often works fine from an ordinary browser
      or residential IP; the publisher's bot protection is refusing automated clients like ours.
      We only assign this on a confirmed signature — an ambiguous HTML response stays
      <code>discovery_parse_error</code>, and a dropped connection stays <code>unreachable</code>,
      because those can't be reliably attributed to a WAF.</dd>

    <dt><code>discovery_blocked</code></dt>
    <dd>Every attempt to fetch a sitemap or RSS feed returned an HTTP error (e.g. 403/404/500)
      with <em>no</em> recognizable WAF fingerprint — typically a CDN or origin that distinguishes
      the site's root from its feed paths, or an access rule we can't attribute to a specific
      bot-management vendor. (Confirmed WAF challenges are reported separately as
      <code>blocked_by_waf</code> above.)</dd>

    <dt><code>discovery_parse_error</code></dt>
    <dd>A sitemap or feed URL returned HTTP 200 but the body wasn't valid XML / RSS / Atom.
      Usually means the publisher's URL routing returns the HTML index page for unknown paths
      (SPA-style catch-all), or the feed is genuinely malformed.</dd>

    <dt><code>no_articles_found</code></dt>
    <dd>Sitemap or feed was reachable and parsed successfully, but contained no articles within
      our publication-date sampling window (most recent 30 days). Typical for sites that publish
      infrequently or whose sitemap only lists section landing pages rather than individual
      articles.</dd>

    <dt><code>unreachable</code></dt>
    <dd>We couldn't establish a connection at all — DNS resolution failed, the TCP connection
      was refused, or the TLS handshake didn't complete. The site may be down, may have
      relocated, or may be blocking our IP range entirely.</dd>

    <dt><code>timeout</code></dt>
    <dd>The server accepted our connection but didn't return a complete response within the
      crawl's per-site time budget. Usually indicates an overloaded origin or a CDN that hangs
      requests from unfamiliar user-agents.</dd>
  </dl>
</Layout>

<style>
  .status-glossary {
    margin: 1rem 0 2rem;
    max-width: 75ch;
  }
  .status-glossary dt {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .status-glossary dt code {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    padding: 1px 6px;
    background: var(--iptc-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--iptc-border);
    border-radius: 3px;
  }
  .status-glossary dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
    color: var(--iptc-muted);
  }
</style>
